{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfs4GJ8TmDPz3WWqt4zu6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I implemented `LRUCache` in `cachelib/lru.py` and added `tests/test_lru.py`. All 20 tests pass (`python3 -m pytest tests -q`). I did not commit.\n\nChanges:\n- **`cachelib/lru.py`**: built on `collections.OrderedDict`, storing `(value, expires_at)` pairs. `get`, `put` and `delete` are O(1) average.\n- **`tests/test_lru.py`**: covers LRU eviction, recency on `get`/`put`, `in` not changing recency, TTL expiry, stats accounting, and capacity validation. Tests use an injected fake clock, so they are deterministic.\n\nChoices I made where the spec was silent, so you can redirect me:\n- **`in` (`__contains__`)** removes an expired entry it finds and counts it as an expiration. It does not touch hits or misses.\n- **`len()`** purges expired entries before counting. This is an O(n) worst case, which departs from the \"O(1) average\" rule for that one method. The alternative is an O(1) `len` that can count entries already past their TTL.\n- **Eviction of an expired LRU entry** on `put` counts as an expiration, not an eviction, per the spec's rule that expired entries are not evictions.\n- **`put` on an existing key** resets its TTL.\n- **`ttl=None`** means entries never expire. I did not add validation for a zero or negative `ttl`.\n\nOne of my own tests initially asserted the wrong expiration count. I corrected the test to match the spec, not the implementation. I removed the generated `__pycache__` and `.pytest_cache` directories afterward."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8472,"cache_read_input_tokens":53701,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8472},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"contex